00:08:22.100 --> 00:08:24.260
So I'll give it back
to Jaques to tell us

00:08:24.260 --> 00:08:25.992
what we've done so far.

00:08:25.992 --> 00:08:27.450
JAQUES CLAPAUCH:
So what did we do?

00:08:27.450 --> 00:08:30.650
We set up GCP using our
super admin account.

00:08:30.650 --> 00:08:33.289
We synced our user
identities using AD

00:08:33.289 --> 00:08:37.669
and now we have that
direct connectivity there.

00:08:37.669 --> 00:08:39.289
And we set up our
first resources.

00:08:39.289 --> 00:08:40.030
We now have VMs.

00:08:40.030 --> 00:08:40.730
We have buckets.

00:08:40.730 --> 00:08:45.050
And we have the raw access
control rules for them.

00:08:45.050 --> 00:08:47.420
But not everything
is perfect right now.

00:08:47.420 --> 00:08:49.199
Now we have some concerns.

00:08:49.199 --> 00:08:50.990
We set everything up
using the super admin.

00:08:50.990 --> 00:08:53.270
And he has very,
very large powers.

00:08:53.270 --> 00:08:56.840
So how can we make sure that
that can't be compromised,

00:08:56.840 --> 00:08:58.730
that a third party can't
gain access to that

00:08:58.730 --> 00:09:01.070
and do evil things, or if,
you know, the super admin

00:09:01.070 --> 00:09:04.340
can't do something by mistake?

00:09:04.340 --> 00:09:07.040
We do have all those resources.

00:09:07.040 --> 00:09:08.750
And they are accessible
by Two Sigma.

00:09:08.750 --> 00:09:11.400
But they're also accessible
outside Two Sigma.

00:09:11.400 --> 00:09:13.329
And that isn't necessarily
something we want.

00:09:13.329 --> 00:09:14.870
We want something
that our developers

00:09:14.870 --> 00:09:18.600
can use but not elsewhere.

00:09:18.600 --> 00:09:23.030
And again, all resources now
can be accessed everywhere.

00:09:23.030 --> 00:09:25.130
We don't really have
any block, any firewall,

00:09:25.130 --> 00:09:29.430
anything prohibiting them
from using it elsewhere.

00:09:29.430 --> 00:09:32.180
And finally, our developers
want to use Kerberos.

00:09:32.180 --> 00:09:34.730
They want to sign into
things directly using it.

00:09:34.730 --> 00:09:36.680
And we currently don't
really have a way

00:09:36.680 --> 00:09:39.800
to sync that through our AD.

00:09:39.800 --> 00:09:42.389
So Sam will tell us how
to resolve those issues.

00:09:42.389 --> 00:09:44.180
SAMPATH SRINIVAS: So
the first point Jaques

00:09:44.180 --> 00:09:46.970
made was the super
admin privilege

00:09:46.970 --> 00:09:49.460
is pretty scary because this
is like a God privilege.

00:09:49.460 --> 00:09:51.050
And you always
need that in the--

00:09:51.050 --> 00:09:52.820
you're setting up your cloud.

00:09:52.820 --> 00:09:55.800
You're always going to have
some foundational account which

00:09:55.800 --> 00:09:56.300
has set up.

00:09:56.300 --> 00:09:58.182
You want to reduce it
to as few as possible,

00:09:58.182 --> 00:09:59.890
but you're still going
to have something.

00:09:59.890 --> 00:10:02.480
And that's not going to
be necessarily on your SSO

00:10:02.480 --> 00:10:06.660
because this is essentially
a bootstrap sccount.

00:10:06.660 --> 00:10:08.940
So how can we make
that more secure?

00:10:08.940 --> 00:10:16.910
So the big concern you have with
account security is phishing.

00:10:16.910 --> 00:10:18.410
Of course, you might
put a password.

00:10:18.410 --> 00:10:19.660
Obviously, there's a password.

00:10:19.660 --> 00:10:21.380
You might put an OTP on this.

00:10:21.380 --> 00:10:22.585
That's very good progress.

00:10:22.585 --> 00:10:24.710
But for something which
could potentially shut down

00:10:24.710 --> 00:10:26.418
in production systems
or whatever, that's

00:10:26.418 --> 00:10:28.190
the kind of privileges
this admin has,

00:10:28.190 --> 00:10:29.990
we'd want to lock down further.

00:10:29.990 --> 00:10:31.880
And what Two Sigma
has done is it's

00:10:31.880 --> 00:10:35.030
used an MFA protocol,
the security key,

00:10:35.030 --> 00:10:38.930
and shut down and locked down
the sessions to be very short.

00:10:38.930 --> 00:10:41.970
And let me tell you a little
bit more about security key.

00:10:41.970 --> 00:10:45.360
So as I said, phishing is
the number one log in attack.

00:10:45.360 --> 00:10:48.050
However, sophisticated
user might be--

00:10:48.050 --> 00:10:49.970
there typically is
a very crafted way

00:10:49.970 --> 00:10:51.650
where when they're
not paying attention,

00:10:51.650 --> 00:10:53.180
you can actually
try and phish them

00:10:53.180 --> 00:10:55.010
for their login credentials.

00:10:55.010 --> 00:10:57.680
And any, what's called
a better credential--

00:10:57.680 --> 00:10:59.930
think anything which you
can type somewhere-- you can

00:10:59.930 --> 00:11:01.138
be typing in the wrong place.

00:11:01.138 --> 00:11:03.835
So that obviously applies to
passwords, applies to OTPs.

00:11:03.835 --> 00:11:05.930
Now we're not trying to
sell OTPs down the river.

00:11:05.930 --> 00:11:07.670
OTPs are a huge step up.

00:11:07.670 --> 00:11:11.200
But for the what's at stake
here, we have to go further.

00:11:11.200 --> 00:11:13.330
And what the security
key gives you

00:11:13.330 --> 00:11:17.080
is essentially a second
factor specifically designed

00:11:17.080 --> 00:11:18.820
to defeat phishing.

00:11:18.820 --> 00:11:20.790
Now defeat is a strong word.

00:11:20.790 --> 00:11:24.240
Nothing is perfect, but it
inherently has this design.

00:11:24.240 --> 00:11:26.800
And so how does that work?

00:11:26.800 --> 00:11:28.441
Let's talk about user
experience first.

00:11:28.441 --> 00:11:30.190
Real security key,
you're the super admin,

00:11:30.190 --> 00:11:32.230
you type in, login password.

00:11:32.230 --> 00:11:35.140
You stick a key into USB
port and press a button.

00:11:35.140 --> 00:11:37.540
What's happening behind the
covers on the server side

00:11:37.540 --> 00:11:40.520
when it verifies the second
factor are two things.

00:11:40.520 --> 00:11:42.390
Firstly, each of these
keys-- a crypto key,

00:11:42.390 --> 00:11:45.460
it has a private keypad
only to the account,

00:11:45.460 --> 00:11:47.380
so of course, it verifies
that, hey, here's

00:11:47.380 --> 00:11:52.630
a signature from this particular
account, which is a key which

00:11:52.630 --> 00:11:54.130
is attached to this account.

00:11:54.130 --> 00:11:55.960
Secondly, the
signature area, what

00:11:55.960 --> 00:11:58.570
is within the signed
boundary, also

00:11:58.570 --> 00:12:01.150
has what the URL
that the browser

00:12:01.150 --> 00:12:04.720
is seeing in actually
where you type over there,

00:12:04.720 --> 00:12:06.457
basically the eTLD.

00:12:06.457 --> 00:12:07.540
That's a pretty key thing.

00:12:07.540 --> 00:12:11.920
Effectively, the server can see
where did your type in the log

00:12:11.920 --> 00:12:12.630
in and password?

00:12:12.630 --> 00:12:15.750
Did you type it at Google.com
or fake Google.com.

00:12:15.750 --> 00:12:19.460
And in fact, that is what
prevents the phishing angle.

00:12:19.460 --> 00:12:22.850
And so it's a very significant
upgrade to security.

00:12:22.850 --> 00:12:24.830
And so the foundational
super admin log ins

00:12:24.830 --> 00:12:26.879
are locked down this way.

00:12:26.879 --> 00:12:29.170
JAQUES CLAPAUCH: And you also
get one really nice bonus

00:12:29.170 --> 00:12:29.669
from this.

00:12:29.669 --> 00:12:32.140
And it's an unwarranted
bonus from the key.

00:12:32.140 --> 00:12:36.670
But basically now, you
can have multi-party off.

00:12:36.670 --> 00:12:39.460
The idea is, if you can share
your password with one party,

00:12:39.460 --> 00:12:41.350
and you can share the
key with another party,

00:12:41.350 --> 00:12:44.680
suddenly you can make sure
that anytime someone logs

00:12:44.680 --> 00:12:46.210
into that account,
it needs to have

00:12:46.210 --> 00:12:49.480
both of those people in, kind of
like in those old movies where

00:12:49.480 --> 00:12:52.962
two people had to turn the key
to launch the launches, yeah.

00:12:52.962 --> 00:12:53.920
SAMPATH SRINIVAS: Yeah.

00:12:53.920 --> 00:12:57.280
In fact, that's a
very interesting use

00:12:57.280 --> 00:13:00.280
which Two Sigma made,
which typically, you

00:13:00.280 --> 00:13:02.809
might say that's great and
all, this add super admin,

00:13:02.809 --> 00:13:03.850
make it even more secure.

00:13:03.850 --> 00:13:07.210
I don't want just a Joe or
Alice to have power to do this.

00:13:07.210 --> 00:13:10.150
They might go rogue on me,
or they might make a mistake.

00:13:10.150 --> 00:13:11.950
They've effectively
used this feature

00:13:11.950 --> 00:13:13.325
to separate out the privileges.

00:13:13.325 --> 00:13:17.550
So Joe's got the password,
and Alice has got the key.

00:13:17.550 --> 00:13:20.127
And as a minor point, you can
actually have multiple keys

00:13:20.127 --> 00:13:20.710
to an account.

00:13:20.710 --> 00:13:25.360
So you can have Alice and
Jane and Bob, perhaps,

00:13:25.360 --> 00:13:29.870
if you so choose, any two
of them kind of thing.

00:13:29.870 --> 00:13:35.077
So going back to what
Jaques had said, he said,

00:13:35.077 --> 00:13:37.660
well, there are various things
wrong, which I want to improve.

00:13:37.660 --> 00:13:39.790
One thing was locking
down the admin account.

00:13:39.790 --> 00:13:43.225
The next thing which he
pointed out was initially

00:13:43.225 --> 00:13:46.510
we set up, for
example, the App Engine

00:13:46.510 --> 00:13:48.580
to be accessed only by DevOps.

00:13:48.580 --> 00:13:51.430
But you can also
have that App Engine

00:13:51.430 --> 00:13:54.370
instance to be
accessed or controlled

00:13:54.370 --> 00:13:56.110
by any identity in Google.

00:13:56.110 --> 00:13:57.370
That's a feature, not a bug.

00:13:57.370 --> 00:13:59.410
For example, in
your case, you might

00:13:59.410 --> 00:14:03.490
say we want this contractor
or that company which

00:14:03.490 --> 00:14:06.980
we have a deal with to be
able to control our resources.

00:14:06.980 --> 00:14:10.820
However, in their case, they
want to have a lockdown.

00:14:10.820 --> 00:14:14.410
They want to ensure that
these resources cannot be even

00:14:14.410 --> 00:14:18.820
accidentally allowed access to
anybody other than an identity

00:14:18.820 --> 00:14:20.230
from Two Sigma.

00:14:20.230 --> 00:14:22.930
And so here again,
they enable something

00:14:22.930 --> 00:14:25.450
which is what was called
an org wide policy.

00:14:25.450 --> 00:14:27.850
So you got your
organization, the container

00:14:27.850 --> 00:14:29.620
in which all the
resources live, and you

00:14:29.620 --> 00:14:32.260
can put an org wide policy,
which essentially says

00:14:32.260 --> 00:14:35.760
in plain English, allow
only Two Sigma entities

00:14:35.760 --> 00:14:38.290
to be having control on these.

00:14:38.290 --> 00:14:45.000
And so what that really does
is basically prevent any kind

00:14:45.000 --> 00:14:47.140
of inadvertent mistakes.

00:14:47.140 --> 00:14:49.500
So for example, someone in
a project, a junior engineer

00:14:49.500 --> 00:14:53.030
project, somebody he or she
might have a bright idea

00:14:53.030 --> 00:14:56.340
to just share some data directly
with an external contractor

00:14:56.340 --> 00:14:57.562
with all good intention.

00:14:57.562 --> 00:14:59.020
But this essentially
prevents that.

00:15:01.700 --> 00:15:05.180
So there are other org
wide policies, too,

00:15:05.180 --> 00:15:06.950
which Two Sigma chose to use.

00:15:06.950 --> 00:15:11.000
And again this might work or
would be useful for you also.

00:15:11.000 --> 00:15:15.890
And in their case, for example,
they set up a policy where a VM

00:15:15.890 --> 00:15:17.420
image, which is
used in one project,

00:15:17.420 --> 00:15:19.080
cannot be shared
with another project.

00:15:19.080 --> 00:15:21.080
And Jaques, maybe you
can tell us a little bit

00:15:21.080 --> 00:15:22.870
about the motivation here.

00:15:22.870 --> 00:15:24.380
JAQUES CLAPAUCH:
So this is an issue

00:15:24.380 --> 00:15:25.505
for which we were pioneers.

00:15:28.640 --> 00:15:30.440
We found out
firsthand that Google

00:15:30.440 --> 00:15:33.620
had added a specific feature
that allowed you to move images

00:15:33.620 --> 00:15:35.270
from within projects,
which is actually

00:15:35.270 --> 00:15:37.440
quite a useful feature,
because in a lot of cases,

00:15:37.440 --> 00:15:38.750
you do want to do that.

00:15:38.750 --> 00:15:40.760
For us, however, that's
an exfiltration vector.

00:15:40.760 --> 00:15:43.450
That means that we can have
a very locked down project

00:15:43.450 --> 00:15:45.634
and a non-locked down
project within the same org.

00:15:45.634 --> 00:15:47.300
And suddenly we can
copy that image that

00:15:47.300 --> 00:15:49.970
has all our private IP
to the other project

00:15:49.970 --> 00:15:51.620
and then take everything out.

00:15:51.620 --> 00:15:53.750
And we came to Google
with that problem.

00:15:53.750 --> 00:15:56.770
And we said, you know, we
don't necessarily want that.

00:15:56.770 --> 00:15:59.180
And Google came around and
made us this specific org

00:15:59.180 --> 00:16:01.400
to help us to specifically
block that, which

00:16:01.400 --> 00:16:03.052
is a great boon to Googles.

00:16:03.052 --> 00:16:04.010
SAMPATH SRINIVAS: Yeah.

00:16:04.010 --> 00:16:06.890
So like Jaques
said, in their case,

00:16:06.890 --> 00:16:10.820
they have multiple kinds
of stakeholders and users.

00:16:10.820 --> 00:16:14.030
And then there were different
policies relevant to each.

00:16:14.030 --> 00:16:15.900
And this prevents the
information sharing

00:16:15.900 --> 00:16:18.650
at the very image level.

00:16:18.650 --> 00:16:21.530
So going on to one more
again, it is the team here

00:16:21.530 --> 00:16:23.190
being org wide control.

00:16:23.190 --> 00:16:25.000
You set them up at
organization level.

00:16:25.000 --> 00:16:27.170
One of the control that
they chose to set up

00:16:27.170 --> 00:16:31.195
is that they allowed no
serial port access to VMs.

00:16:31.195 --> 00:16:33.259
Serial port access to
VMs, again, is a feature.

00:16:33.259 --> 00:16:35.550
It might be in your case,
you might want to allow that.

00:16:35.550 --> 00:16:36.883
But in their case, they did not.

00:16:36.883 --> 00:16:39.230
And just to recap,
you bring up a VM.

00:16:39.230 --> 00:16:40.985
If it's on the network,
you can SSH to it.

00:16:40.985 --> 00:16:43.110
But then, there might be
some network config issue.

00:16:43.110 --> 00:16:44.810
It might not be accessible.

00:16:44.810 --> 00:16:46.640
The serial port is
the sub network.

00:16:46.640 --> 00:16:48.810
You're accessing it
in course directly.

00:16:48.810 --> 00:16:50.270
And that's what we allow.

00:16:50.270 --> 00:16:52.950
But in their case, they
chose not to allow that.

00:16:52.950 --> 00:16:55.790
And Jaques, maybe you can
tell us what drove that.

00:16:55.790 --> 00:16:59.390
JAQUES CLAPAUCH: Sure, so again,
that access is unrestricted.

00:16:59.390 --> 00:17:00.119
It is useful.

00:17:00.119 --> 00:17:02.660
If you have a network, if you
have a machine that's breaking,

00:17:02.660 --> 00:17:03.820
and you can't get
through the network,

00:17:03.820 --> 00:17:06.040
and you configured
something wrong somewhere,

00:17:06.040 --> 00:17:07.970
and you really have
to get to that VM,

00:17:07.970 --> 00:17:09.020
this is the way to do it.

00:17:09.020 --> 00:17:10.548
And it's useful.

00:17:10.548 --> 00:17:12.589
But again, for us, that's
an exfiltration vector.

00:17:12.589 --> 00:17:15.140
It means somebody can forego
all of the network rules

00:17:15.140 --> 00:17:17.854
and just go directly into
your project, into your VM,

00:17:17.854 --> 00:17:19.520
and take everything
out, as long as they

00:17:19.520 --> 00:17:22.400
know the password, which I'm
sure malicious people can get.

00:17:22.400 --> 00:17:25.397
So we wanted to block that.

00:17:25.397 --> 00:17:27.230
SAMPATH SRINIVAS: So
let's take a little bit

00:17:27.230 --> 00:17:28.890
of a stock of where we are.

00:17:28.890 --> 00:17:31.280
So we have set up an
integrated set of policies.

00:17:31.280 --> 00:17:33.440
Every one of those
yellow lines that you

00:17:33.440 --> 00:17:36.840
see, it represents basically
a policy which we-- you

00:17:36.840 --> 00:17:37.970
saw us setting up.

00:17:37.970 --> 00:17:41.850
So at the App Engine instance,
the Compute Engine instances,

00:17:41.850 --> 00:17:44.430
the Cloud Storage, we set up
policies, the group level,

00:17:44.430 --> 00:17:45.530
as you remember.

00:17:45.530 --> 00:17:47.960
The super admins
are locked down so

00:17:47.960 --> 00:17:50.840
that they can only have a
phishing resistant log in,

00:17:50.840 --> 00:17:52.250
have short sessions.

00:17:52.250 --> 00:17:54.230
The admin uses
identity effectively.

00:17:54.230 --> 00:17:59.060
They are delegated down
from active vectoring.

00:17:59.060 --> 00:18:00.350
That's the policy.

00:18:00.350 --> 00:18:03.750
And the box around a Two Sigma
organization-- if you remember,

00:18:03.750 --> 00:18:06.170
we set policies saying
that you can't share

00:18:06.170 --> 00:18:08.090
ID resources from outside.

00:18:08.090 --> 00:18:09.560
Your can't copy images.

00:18:09.560 --> 00:18:14.100
You can't lock down
serial port and so forth.

00:18:14.100 --> 00:18:16.557
So let's take some stock here.

00:18:16.557 --> 00:18:18.140
And I'll give that
back to Jaques now.

00:18:18.140 --> 00:18:21.500
JAQUES CLAPAUCH: So we now lock
down our super admin log in.

00:18:21.500 --> 00:18:25.880
Super admins now don't
have god-like powers.

00:18:25.880 --> 00:18:29.630
We now didn't allow resources to
be shared outside of Two Sigma

00:18:29.630 --> 00:18:30.284
identities.

00:18:30.284 --> 00:18:31.700
So people outside
Two Sigma really

00:18:31.700 --> 00:18:34.340
can't access anything yet.

00:18:34.340 --> 00:18:36.890
But we still haven't
solved everything.

00:18:36.890 --> 00:18:39.230
Resources can still be
accessed from anywhere.

00:18:39.230 --> 00:18:42.140
And we still don't have our much
desired Kerberos for our deps.

00:18:42.140 --> 00:18:45.830
And Sam's going to walk us
through how we get that.

00:18:45.830 --> 00:18:49.430
SAMPATH SRINIVAS: So network
layer control, as all of us

00:18:49.430 --> 00:18:54.090
know, we want multiple
layers of access controls.

00:18:54.090 --> 00:18:59.346
And one conservative and obvious
access control mechanism is

00:18:59.346 --> 00:19:02.060
to put a network boundary around
a set of resources and make

00:19:02.060 --> 00:19:05.300
sure they cannot be accessed
from anywhere but there.

00:19:05.300 --> 00:19:07.400
So that's called a VPC.

00:19:07.400 --> 00:19:09.560
And in this slide, we
show the first step

00:19:09.560 --> 00:19:13.100
of a VPC, which is
around the Linux VMs.

00:19:13.100 --> 00:19:17.330
And there it's the
classic network construct.

00:19:17.330 --> 00:19:19.730
You have a bunch
of Linux machines,

00:19:19.730 --> 00:19:21.710
and you put, basically,
a firewall around them.

00:19:21.710 --> 00:19:24.680
And they cannot open a VPC
connection or SSH or whatever,

00:19:24.680 --> 00:19:26.240
SAP out from the boundary.

00:19:26.240 --> 00:19:27.980
And similarly, they
are private IPs.

00:19:27.980 --> 00:19:30.080
They can't be accessed
from the outside.

00:19:30.080 --> 00:19:30.950
That's interesting.

00:19:30.950 --> 00:19:34.280
But we want to bring
that notion of VPC

00:19:34.280 --> 00:19:38.870
not just to just a translation
of the physical pass,

00:19:38.870 --> 00:19:42.170
but also to these virtual
constructs or these Cloud

00:19:42.170 --> 00:19:45.980
constructs, which is the App
Engine and the Cloud storage.

00:19:45.980 --> 00:19:47.180
And how do we do that?

00:19:47.180 --> 00:19:50.475
So what is a mechanism
for doing so?

00:19:50.475 --> 00:19:52.850
And so essentially, we want
to say these things cannot be

00:19:52.850 --> 00:19:54.900
accessed from the outside.

00:19:54.900 --> 00:19:59.960
So there again, as you can
imagine, it's in the Cloud.

00:19:59.960 --> 00:20:02.160
Everything is policy
and config driven.

00:20:02.160 --> 00:20:05.690
And on the VPC, you
set a resource policy

00:20:05.690 --> 00:20:06.980
to do precisely that.

00:20:06.980 --> 00:20:10.190
So in effect, here the
plain English notion

00:20:10.190 --> 00:20:13.160
is you can't access data from
the outside on a containing

00:20:13.160 --> 00:20:15.275
VPC, from outside
the containing VPC.

00:20:15.275 --> 00:20:19.130
And what that really means is
that that App Engine instance,

00:20:19.130 --> 00:20:23.180
the Cloud storage instance, now
that management or the access

00:20:23.180 --> 00:20:25.880
to the APIs, if you remember
before, they were locked down

00:20:25.880 --> 00:20:27.565
to the dev ops
group or whatever,

00:20:27.565 --> 00:20:29.690
the dev ops group can no
longer do it from anywhere

00:20:29.690 --> 00:20:30.380
on the internet.

00:20:30.380 --> 00:20:33.090
They can only do it
if their source IP

00:20:33.090 --> 00:20:36.360
are within the private network.

00:20:36.360 --> 00:20:38.780
So in effect, bringing
these abstractions

00:20:38.780 --> 00:20:40.610
which Google is
providing you also

00:20:40.610 --> 00:20:43.730
within the notion of
a network boundary.

00:20:43.730 --> 00:20:49.130
So having said that,
the next step we do

00:20:49.130 --> 00:20:53.462
is this VPC obviously is
like what you would expect.

00:20:53.462 --> 00:20:58.520
It is a private network and you
connect it up to your on prem.

00:20:58.520 --> 00:21:02.930
And what Two Sigma chose to do
is there are multiple options.

00:21:02.930 --> 00:21:05.720
You can use either a site to
site VPN or something called

00:21:05.720 --> 00:21:07.127
a private interconnect.

00:21:07.127 --> 00:21:08.960
And they chose to use
a private interconnect

00:21:08.960 --> 00:21:10.900
and that gives them
some amazing speeds,

00:21:10.900 --> 00:21:12.950
a 60 gigabit connection.

00:21:12.950 --> 00:21:15.140
So in this context, we're
talking about security,

00:21:15.140 --> 00:21:17.580
but it's also
really, really fast.

00:21:17.580 --> 00:21:19.580
So the connection, once
you connected up to your

00:21:19.580 --> 00:21:23.420
on premise, your VPC,
which is on the Cloud,

00:21:23.420 --> 00:21:26.770
that brings us to the solution
for the fourth problem which

00:21:26.770 --> 00:21:27.750
Jaques talked about.

00:21:27.750 --> 00:21:31.850
Which is basically that the
developers we used before when

00:21:31.850 --> 00:21:34.960
on prem, if you see the
local compute on the on prem,

00:21:34.960 --> 00:21:37.280
they were used to getting a
Kerberos ticket from Active

00:21:37.280 --> 00:21:41.690
Directory and logging into
their Linux machines on prem.

00:21:41.690 --> 00:21:44.120
They want the exact
same thing, basically,

00:21:44.120 --> 00:21:47.030
on the virtual machines
which are now on Cloud.

00:21:47.030 --> 00:21:49.676
And this, when you hook
up the network this way,

00:21:49.676 --> 00:21:51.050
you have the
private connectivity

00:21:51.050 --> 00:21:53.940
and the Kerberos ticket now
can travel over the network.

00:21:53.940 --> 00:21:56.800
And so you can get
Kerberos access to the VMs.

00:21:56.800 --> 00:22:01.591
And so that basically gets us
to where the list of questions

00:22:01.591 --> 00:22:02.090
we had.

00:22:02.090 --> 00:22:03.740
And I'll give this
back to Jaques here.

00:22:03.740 --> 00:22:06.080
JAQUES CLAPAUCH: So as
we talked about before,

00:22:06.080 --> 00:22:07.730
we had already
solved one and two.

00:22:07.730 --> 00:22:09.650
So on to three and four.

00:22:09.650 --> 00:22:11.120
We now have the VPC.

00:22:11.120 --> 00:22:15.620
So now data can't be easily
exfiltrated from that.

00:22:15.620 --> 00:22:17.702
And we enabled Kerberos,
so now our developers

00:22:17.702 --> 00:22:19.160
can just sign in
to things directly

00:22:19.160 --> 00:22:22.220
without all that hassle.

00:22:22.220 --> 00:22:25.580
But we're still
not 100% there yet.

00:22:25.580 --> 00:22:28.830
So a bad insider can't copy
the data out of the network.

00:22:28.830 --> 00:22:30.460
We locked it down, right?

00:22:30.460 --> 00:22:33.080
But we only did it directly.

00:22:33.080 --> 00:22:35.660
What if someone
indirectly takes the data,

00:22:35.660 --> 00:22:37.700
copies it to another
GCP resource,

00:22:37.700 --> 00:22:40.160
and takes it out from
that open GCP resource,

00:22:40.160 --> 00:22:42.870
like a personal bucket?

00:22:42.870 --> 00:22:46.280
So buckets have encryption
by default, which is good.

00:22:46.280 --> 00:22:48.930
But we have compliance issues.

00:22:48.930 --> 00:22:51.740
And we need to manage the keys
so we can give good compliance

00:22:51.740 --> 00:22:52.890
visibility.

00:22:52.890 --> 00:22:55.010
And right now, no
resources can be

00:22:55.010 --> 00:22:58.370
accessed from outside the
VPC, but sometimes you

00:22:58.370 --> 00:22:59.390
want to give access.

00:22:59.390 --> 00:23:01.640
We want people from the
internet to have access

00:23:01.640 --> 00:23:02.670
to some of our data.

00:23:02.670 --> 00:23:05.810
So how can we do that
securely in a way that doesn't

00:23:05.810 --> 00:23:07.550
break what we have right now?

00:23:07.550 --> 00:23:09.780
And Sam's going to
talk about that.

00:23:09.780 --> 00:23:11.450
SAMPATH SRINIVAS:
Thanks, Jaques.

00:23:11.450 --> 00:23:14.920
So the first problem that
Jaques talked about was,

00:23:14.920 --> 00:23:17.340
do we set up a VPC boundary?

00:23:17.340 --> 00:23:20.840
And though it was the case that
that App Engine instance now

00:23:20.840 --> 00:23:25.250
cannot be accessed by Joe in
dev ops unless Joe in dev ops is

00:23:25.250 --> 00:23:31.230
within the VPC network, so to
speak, there's still a problem.

00:23:31.230 --> 00:23:35.610
Which is that, if Joe
goes rogue, in principle,

00:23:35.610 --> 00:23:38.660
or even Joe, by making
an inadvertent mistake,

00:23:38.660 --> 00:23:43.430
could set up instance
of a personal bucket

00:23:43.430 --> 00:23:47.440
and could make a copy from
the data which is inside

00:23:47.440 --> 00:23:49.220
a VPC to the personal bucket.

00:23:49.220 --> 00:23:52.301
In essence, we don't
have an outbound--

00:23:52.301 --> 00:23:54.050
at that point, at this
point of the build,

00:23:54.050 --> 00:23:56.510
we don't have an outbound
firewall, so to speak,

00:23:56.510 --> 00:23:58.460
which prevents
you from accessing

00:23:58.460 --> 00:24:02.360
any resource within GCP,
a GCP resource which

00:24:02.360 --> 00:24:04.680
is not within your VPC.

00:24:04.680 --> 00:24:08.000
So this precisely now
what we add as a policy.

00:24:08.000 --> 00:24:13.520
So we add a policy saying, no
GCP resources outside my VPC

00:24:13.520 --> 00:24:16.740
can be accessed
from inside the VPC.

00:24:16.740 --> 00:24:19.160
So you can think of, for
example all Cloud buckets

00:24:19.160 --> 00:24:22.500
and GCP now broken
into two sets.

00:24:22.500 --> 00:24:24.460
One is the sets which
are within your VPC,

00:24:24.460 --> 00:24:27.577
as you have designated,
and one is everything else.

00:24:27.577 --> 00:24:28.910
It doesn't matter who owns them.

00:24:28.910 --> 00:24:31.140
It could be your organization
or somebody else.

00:24:31.140 --> 00:24:35.810
Now those, even though the
API endpoint for, obviously,

00:24:35.810 --> 00:24:38.090
Cloud services are accessible,
you cannot access those

00:24:38.090 --> 00:24:39.240
particular resources.

00:24:39.240 --> 00:24:43.110
And so this cuts out this
huge exfiltration vector.

00:24:43.110 --> 00:24:47.660
And you can think of this as a
completion step, where you're

00:24:47.660 --> 00:24:52.760
truly bringing just your Cloud
resources within your network

00:24:52.760 --> 00:24:54.960
boundary.

00:24:54.960 --> 00:24:57.620
And this is feature, in
case you're looking for it,

00:24:57.620 --> 00:25:00.720
is called VPC service
control and is

00:25:00.720 --> 00:25:06.820
something that you might want
to enable in your setup also.

00:25:06.820 --> 00:25:09.720
The next point which
Jaques talked about

00:25:09.720 --> 00:25:17.950
was that, if you use our
storage offerings, by default,

00:25:17.950 --> 00:25:19.950
all data's encrypted at best.

00:25:19.950 --> 00:25:23.700
And we manage the keys, or
Google manages the keys.

00:25:23.700 --> 00:25:26.950
But for visibility or for
compliance, in your case,

00:25:26.950 --> 00:25:30.000
you might say, I want
that key management.

00:25:30.000 --> 00:25:33.849
And luckily, Google
provides a feature for that,

00:25:33.849 --> 00:25:35.140
with the Cloud key management--

00:25:35.140 --> 00:25:38.280
KMS feature.

00:25:38.280 --> 00:25:41.010
And so what happens is that
you set up an instance of Cloud

00:25:41.010 --> 00:25:45.330
KMS, and in this case, was Two
Sigma to manage their KMS keys.

00:25:45.330 --> 00:25:46.780
And again, it's policy driven.

00:25:46.780 --> 00:25:48.820
You're effectively--
in essence, you

00:25:48.820 --> 00:25:51.620
are setting the Cloud
storage instances

00:25:51.620 --> 00:25:53.490
to use keys that
you're managing.

00:25:53.490 --> 00:25:54.520
And that's a policy.

00:25:54.520 --> 00:25:57.840
So once again, it's software
driven, config driven.

00:25:57.840 --> 00:26:01.090
And so at this point, we
have solved the problem

00:26:01.090 --> 00:26:04.210
of the visibility of our
control over the keys,

00:26:04.210 --> 00:26:09.720
which are key management,
which Jaques was looking for.

00:26:09.720 --> 00:26:14.790
And that brings us to the
next point that Jaques made.

00:26:14.790 --> 00:26:18.630
So the only reason you build
systems are for access.

00:26:18.630 --> 00:26:21.540
And in the end, some
subset of users,

00:26:21.540 --> 00:26:24.240
you want them to have community
access, ideally from anywhere

00:26:24.240 --> 00:26:25.590
and safely.

00:26:25.590 --> 00:26:29.410
And so here the end
users in their set

00:26:29.410 --> 00:26:34.230
up could be employees, could
be those external modelers.

00:26:34.230 --> 00:26:38.530
And Jacques told us there
were six classes of users.

00:26:38.530 --> 00:26:39.960
How do we enable--
you don't want

00:26:39.960 --> 00:26:42.001
to bring them all inside
your production network,

00:26:42.001 --> 00:26:43.200
obviously, for access.

00:26:43.200 --> 00:26:45.660
You want to keep them
outside, but give them

00:26:45.660 --> 00:26:48.820
safe access to what
you want to publish.

00:26:48.820 --> 00:26:53.520
And that's precisely
what the IAP allows.

00:26:53.520 --> 00:26:57.750
So IAP stands for
identity of a proxy.

00:26:57.750 --> 00:27:01.350
So it is essentially
a reverse proxy front

00:27:01.350 --> 00:27:06.550
end, which is internet facing
for your back end application.

00:27:06.550 --> 00:27:09.000
If you remember, we talked
about this back end App Engine

00:27:09.000 --> 00:27:10.000
application.

00:27:10.000 --> 00:27:12.990
And as I said, that's
a web application

00:27:12.990 --> 00:27:16.620
written by Two Sigma to
essentially provide a unified

00:27:16.620 --> 00:27:18.810
front end to all the
complex processing happening

00:27:18.810 --> 00:27:21.310
at the back model
running and so on.

00:27:21.310 --> 00:27:24.000
How do you take that
application and publish it

00:27:24.000 --> 00:27:25.500
to the internet
in a safe fashion?

00:27:25.500 --> 00:27:27.670
That's precisely what IAP does.

00:27:27.670 --> 00:27:32.770
So in this case, you'll
see this name TwoSigma.com.

00:27:32.770 --> 00:27:34.830
They're publishing an
application, TwoSigma.com

00:27:34.830 --> 00:27:36.780
securely to the internet.

00:27:36.780 --> 00:27:39.860
When you come to TwoSigma.com
as a user from the internet,

00:27:39.860 --> 00:27:41.610
the backend application
are hitting.

00:27:41.610 --> 00:27:44.770
Really, this is back end
App Engine application.

00:27:44.770 --> 00:27:49.170
However, there's a
bit more to that.

00:27:49.170 --> 00:27:51.120
You can essentially write--

00:27:51.120 --> 00:27:55.830
what Two Sigma has done is
use the IAM features of IAP.

00:27:55.830 --> 00:27:58.900
You can write in policies as to
who can access the application.

00:27:58.900 --> 00:28:01.600
So in this case-- we just
made up this example.

00:28:01.600 --> 00:28:03.390
Maybe this TwoSigma.com
application

00:28:03.390 --> 00:28:06.090
can be accessed only by
the contractors group.

00:28:06.090 --> 00:28:07.320
Just say so.

00:28:07.320 --> 00:28:11.430
And what happens is that,
at every HTTPS request,

00:28:11.430 --> 00:28:16.210
the actual user logged
in as validated by IAP.

00:28:16.210 --> 00:28:19.320
And if the policy allows, it
sends the hit to the back end.

00:28:19.320 --> 00:28:22.700
If not, it sends you a
polite page, saying sorry,

00:28:22.700 --> 00:28:24.990
you're not allowed
access to this resource.

00:28:24.990 --> 00:28:28.080
The nice thing about this is
that the back end App Engine

00:28:28.080 --> 00:28:33.550
application was written with
no particular access controls

00:28:33.550 --> 00:28:34.530
baked into it.

00:28:34.530 --> 00:28:35.920
Because you never know.

00:28:35.920 --> 00:28:38.760
Tomorrow you might want some
other group to access it.

00:28:38.760 --> 00:28:40.800
So now your
infrastructure, which

00:28:40.800 --> 00:28:44.360
IAP enables you to essentially
add these access controls

00:28:44.360 --> 00:28:46.950
right in the front
end, and provide you

00:28:46.950 --> 00:28:51.080
just the limited
access that you want.

00:28:51.080 --> 00:28:53.650
So let me give it back to
Jaques to see where we are now

00:28:53.650 --> 00:28:55.090
in this journey.

00:28:55.090 --> 00:28:58.290
JAQUES CLAPAUCH:
So right now, we

00:28:58.290 --> 00:29:01.210
make sure that insiders
can't misuse the resources

00:29:01.210 --> 00:29:04.075
to indirectly take data out.

00:29:04.075 --> 00:29:06.190
We mange the encryption
keys to make sure

00:29:06.190 --> 00:29:08.575
all the storage is
compliant and visible--

00:29:11.170 --> 00:29:13.010
the keys are invisible.

00:29:13.010 --> 00:29:15.250
And we published the end
user applications safely

00:29:15.250 --> 00:29:21.520
to the internet so people can
now use our services outside.

00:29:21.520 --> 00:29:24.880
But of course, we do
have one last issue.

00:29:24.880 --> 00:29:28.420
And as my colleague,
Nick Orfanidis always

00:29:28.420 --> 00:29:32.676
quotes, if you feel
that you're 100% secure

00:29:32.676 --> 00:29:34.050
and you haven't
been broken into,

00:29:34.050 --> 00:29:35.440
you likely have
been broken, into

00:29:35.440 --> 00:29:36.970
and you just don't
know about it.

00:29:36.970 --> 00:29:39.490
So no security is 100% perfect.

00:29:39.490 --> 00:29:42.160
And for the cases where
it actually does go wrong,

00:29:42.160 --> 00:29:44.140
because things do
go wrong, we need

00:29:44.140 --> 00:29:47.350
to be able to figure out
what went wrong, check on it,

00:29:47.350 --> 00:29:48.530
and fix it later.

00:29:48.530 --> 00:29:51.100
So for that, we need status
and we need forensics.

00:29:51.100 --> 00:29:54.359
And for that, we have
a number of tools.

00:29:54.359 --> 00:29:55.900
Initially, we have
Stackdriver, which

00:29:55.900 --> 00:29:59.680
gives us the logs that tells
us who did what when, not why,

00:29:59.680 --> 00:30:01.690
but what and when.

00:30:01.690 --> 00:30:05.490
We have CSCC, which gives
us a console that tells us

00:30:05.490 --> 00:30:07.630
the consolidated
status of everything

00:30:07.630 --> 00:30:10.730
that we have in
our organization.

00:30:10.730 --> 00:30:13.480
And finally, we have Google's
Forseti, with the open source

00:30:13.480 --> 00:30:15.910
tool Forseti, to
automate the monitoring

00:30:15.910 --> 00:30:17.435
and the setting up of policies.

00:30:17.435 --> 00:30:19.060
SAMPATH SRINIVAS:
And I guess one thing

00:30:19.060 --> 00:30:21.170
I should mention for
those who don't know,

00:30:21.170 --> 00:30:24.730
is CSCC stands for Cloud
Security Command Center.

00:30:24.730 --> 00:30:27.340
It's essentially intended
to be a central console,

00:30:27.340 --> 00:30:30.910
very good visibility into
all your security monitoring.

00:30:30.910 --> 00:30:32.860
And Forseti is a set
of open source tools

00:30:32.860 --> 00:30:36.120
that we have offered-- look
at Forseti.org-- specifically

00:30:36.120 --> 00:30:40.540
so you can automate setting
up and monitoring of policies

00:30:40.540 --> 00:30:46.120
in GCP and beyond, actually.

00:30:46.120 --> 00:30:47.870
JAQUES CLAPAUCH: And
finally, we come back

00:30:47.870 --> 00:30:50.424
to our finalized picture
of what we should

00:30:50.424 --> 00:30:52.340
have if you want a secure
environment with all

00:30:52.340 --> 00:30:56.960
the features that Google has.

00:30:56.960 --> 00:30:58.840
So we finally got to
that level that we want.

00:30:58.840 --> 00:31:02.685
We want secure inside,
outside, and we have not built

00:31:02.685 --> 00:31:04.640
any walls for our developers.

00:31:04.640 --> 00:31:06.960
As the quote I
often say, it says,

00:31:06.960 --> 00:31:08.870
build guardrails, not walls.

00:31:08.870 --> 00:31:11.390
So everything is usable,
everything is secure,

00:31:11.390 --> 00:31:15.740
and we did it simply step by
step so you guys can follow it.

00:31:15.740 --> 00:31:18.380
And to sum it up,
Two Sigma-- we're

00:31:18.380 --> 00:31:21.410
a fintech company with extremely
high value IP, like many of you

00:31:21.410 --> 00:31:22.400
might have.

00:31:22.400 --> 00:31:25.170
And as I said, we need
to lock everything down,

00:31:25.170 --> 00:31:27.320
but make access easy.

00:31:27.320 --> 00:31:29.690
And we need a hybrid
solution that spans

00:31:29.690 --> 00:31:31.970
both Cloud and on prem.

00:31:31.970 --> 00:31:34.250
And by using GCP,
we get a control

00:31:34.250 --> 00:31:38.330
to do so securely, easily, and
still use a lot of features.

00:31:38.330 --> 00:31:41.120
And because of them, we're
able to easily stitch

00:31:41.120 --> 00:31:45.150
all of that together
to exact our needs.

00:31:45.150 --> 00:31:49.140
So Sam will tell us how--
where we can go next.

00:31:49.140 --> 00:31:51.290
SAMPATH SRINIVAS: So
the core principle

00:31:51.290 --> 00:31:54.470
in what we were doing, which
really applies to Cloud really,

00:31:54.470 --> 00:31:58.490
is that, as I said,
controlling access is what

00:31:58.490 --> 00:32:00.330
really security is all about.

00:32:00.330 --> 00:32:05.150
And so at every point, we
were using access controls,

00:32:05.150 --> 00:32:09.030
identity driven access controls,
to say who can access what.

00:32:09.030 --> 00:32:11.750
So here when we say who,
it was people things.

00:32:11.750 --> 00:32:15.700
So this group of people
can access this application

00:32:15.700 --> 00:32:17.160
published out to the internet.

00:32:17.160 --> 00:32:20.910
This group of people can set
this App Engine instance,

00:32:20.910 --> 00:32:24.140
but only if they're in
this context, for example,

00:32:24.140 --> 00:32:27.300
from within the
VPC and so forth.

00:32:27.300 --> 00:32:30.140
The next step
essentially is to--

00:32:30.140 --> 00:32:33.350
but however, at this
stage of the conversation,

00:32:33.350 --> 00:32:37.820
when Linux VM called
another Linux VM,

00:32:37.820 --> 00:32:39.810
basically it was
a TCP connection.

00:32:39.810 --> 00:32:41.960
Maybe it called some API.

00:32:41.960 --> 00:32:45.210
But that was not a named
thing at this point.

00:32:45.210 --> 00:32:49.150
And so where Two Sigma is--

00:32:49.150 --> 00:32:52.160
we are having conversations is
to take the notion of identity

00:32:52.160 --> 00:32:54.410
driven access, not
just for human beings,

00:32:54.410 --> 00:32:56.330
but as the services themselves.

00:32:56.330 --> 00:32:59.330
Which I'm sure you've been
listening to the keynotes

00:32:59.330 --> 00:33:02.220
when we talk about Istio and
Apogee, that's what it's about.

00:33:02.220 --> 00:33:08.900
So now every service
actually has a name.

00:33:08.900 --> 00:33:12.050
And you can set policies, which
the platform allows you to do,

00:33:12.050 --> 00:33:14.530
where before you could say
only Joe can access API.

00:33:14.530 --> 00:33:17.870
Now you can say which services
can access what other service.

00:33:17.870 --> 00:33:20.300
And so everything that
gets published is APIs,

00:33:20.300 --> 00:33:23.040
and you actually have
interservice controls

00:33:23.040 --> 00:33:25.280
on a service and
data driven basis.

00:33:25.280 --> 00:33:26.580
And so that's the next stage.

00:33:26.580 --> 00:33:30.800
So you can just think of kicking
the notion of security controls

00:33:30.800 --> 00:33:32.450
further and further
up the stack.

00:33:32.450 --> 00:33:36.020
Not that the lower layers
become obviated by any means.

00:33:36.020 --> 00:33:38.140
The network layers are
extremely important.

00:33:38.140 --> 00:33:41.460
But basically, once
you put them there,

00:33:41.460 --> 00:33:45.142
they are sort of-- so maybe
I use the word "fairly"--

00:33:45.142 --> 00:33:47.600
crude at beginning because they
don't have the higher level

00:33:47.600 --> 00:33:48.100
concepts.

00:33:48.100 --> 00:33:50.930
And then you basically now
focus your security setup

00:33:50.930 --> 00:33:54.140
more of the identity
layers for both individuals

00:33:54.140 --> 00:33:54.950
and for services.

00:33:54.950 --> 00:33:56.770
And that's where
that conversation

00:33:56.770 --> 00:33:59.560
is going with Two Sigma,
and Istio and Apogee.

00:33:59.560 --> 00:34:02.880
And they have been a
partner for us, actually,

00:34:02.880 --> 00:34:07.160
in driving many of the
controls that we put in.

00:34:07.160 --> 00:34:09.469
And we look forward
to their feedback.

00:34:09.469 --> 00:34:11.690
And the same thing and
similarly from all of you--

00:34:11.690 --> 00:34:13.639
please join us in this journey.

00:34:13.639 --> 00:34:15.604
Use what you've
got, and then set up

00:34:15.604 --> 00:34:18.020
all the controls that you have
and tell us what's missing.

00:34:18.020 --> 00:34:19.580
And so help us
drive it where it's

00:34:19.580 --> 00:34:21.533
complete from your perspective.

00:34:21.533 --> 00:34:23.949
There may be other controls
which are not relevant to them

00:34:23.949 --> 00:34:26.159
which you might need
which we are not aware of.

00:34:26.159 --> 00:34:27.409
Let's talk about that.

00:34:27.409 --> 00:34:29.570
JAQUES CLAPAUCH: And I
can't emphasize that enough.

00:34:29.570 --> 00:34:31.940
Always, always work with Google.

00:34:31.940 --> 00:34:34.969
Because no matter where you
go, what you do anywhere,

00:34:34.969 --> 00:34:36.630
nothing will be
100% what you need.

00:34:36.630 --> 00:34:38.130
But working with
Google, you can get

00:34:38.130 --> 00:34:39.679
to that 100% of what you need.

00:34:39.679 --> 00:34:42.220
And they will help, and they're
actually very, very helpful--

00:34:42.220 --> 00:34:45.469
from AMs to PMs to temps
to everyone at Google,

00:34:45.469 --> 00:34:47.389
they really, really
help you drive to where

00:34:47.389 --> 00:34:50.067
your solution needs to go.

00:34:50.067 --> 00:34:51.650
SAMPATH SRINIVAS:
Thank you very much.

00:34:51.650 --> 00:34:52.850
[APPLAUSE]

00:34:52.850 --> 00:34:55.300
[MUSIC PLAYING]

